### How does wait()/notify() work?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class WaitNotifyDemo {
    private final Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    private final int capacity = 10;

    public synchronized void produce(String trade) throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.size() >= capacity) {
            wait(); // releases lock, enters wait-set
        }
        queue.add(trade);
        notifyAll(); // wakes ALL waiters (producers AND consumers)
    }

    public synchronized String consume() throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.isEmpty()) {
            wait(); // releases lock, enters wait-set
        }
        String trade = queue.poll();
        notifyAll(); // wakes ALL waiters
        return trade;
    }
}
```

The problem: `notifyAll()` wakes both producers and consumers. If 50 consumers are waiting and one item is added, all 50 wake up, 49 recheck the condition, and go back to sleep. Wasteful.

### How does Condition.await()/signal() improve this?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class ConditionDemo {
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();
    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();
    private final Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    private final int capacity = 10;

    public void produce(String trade) throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (queue.size() >= capacity) notFull.await();
            queue.add(trade);
            notEmpty.signal(); // wakes ONE consumer only
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }

    public String consume() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (queue.isEmpty()) notEmpty.await();
            String trade = queue.poll();
            notFull.signal(); // wakes ONE producer only
            return trade;
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }
}
```

### How do they compare?

| Feature | wait/notify | Condition |
|---|---|---|
| Lock type | synchronized | ReentrantLock |
| Wait-sets per lock | One | Multiple |
| Targeted wake-up | No (wakes any) | Yes (signal specific Condition) |
| Timeout | wait(millis) | await(time, unit) |
| Interruptible | Always | awaitUninterruptibly() option |
| Must hold lock | Yes (synchronized) | Yes (lock.lock()) |
| Spurious wake-ups | Yes (use while) | Yes (use while) |

### What are the rules for both mechanisms?

1. Must be called while holding the lock (synchronized for wait/notify, lock.lock() for Condition).
2. Always use a `while` loop, never an `if` statement.
3. `wait()` and `await()` release the lock. When they return, the lock is re-acquired.
4. Prefer `notifyAll()` over `notify()` for wait/notify (safer). Use `signal()` with Condition (targeted).

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **LinkedBlockingQueue internally uses two Conditions**: one for `notEmpty` (consumers wait) and one for `notFull` (producers wait). It also uses two separate locks (`takeLock` and `putLock`) for concurrent put and take.
- **Condition.awaitNanos(long)**: returns the remaining time, allowing precise timeout tracking across retry loops.
- **In practice**: use `BlockingQueue` (which handles all the locking and signaling internally) instead of writing your own wait/notify or Condition-based queue.
